```python
class Composition:
    def __init__(self, title, composer, period):
        self.title = title
        self.composer = composer
        self.period = period

class MusicDatabase:
    def __init__(self):
        self.compositions = []

    def add_composition(self, title, composer, period):
        new_composition = Composition(title, composer, period)
        self.compositions.append(new_composition)

    def list_compositions(self):
        for composition in self.compositions:
            print(f"Title: {composition.title}, Composer: {composition.composer}, Period: {composition.period}")

    def search_composition(self, title):
        for composition in self.compositions:
            if composition.title == title:
                return (f"Title: {composition.title}, Composer: {composition.composer}, Period: {composition.period}")
        return "Composition not found"

database = MusicDatabase()
database.add_composition('Symphony No. 5', 'Beethoven', 'Classical')
database.add_composition('The Four Seasons', 'Vivaldi', 'Baroque')

database.list_compositions()
print(database.search_composition('Symphony No. 5'))  # returns the composition data
print(database.search_composition('Unknown'))  # returns 'Composition not found'
```

This program can store musical compositions in a "database" (which is a list, for this simple example). You can add compositions to the database, list all compositions, and search for a composition by title. Please note that in real world scenarios, you would want to use a real database system to store and manage your data. Python supports several database systems (like SQLite, MySQL, PostgreSQL, MongoDB etc.) that you can use. 

For an extensive database, you should use a full-featured database system and possibly also some full-featured web framework, like Django (which comes with its own ORM for database access), Flask (possibly in combination with SQLAlchemy for database access) or Pyramid. Please follow appropriate tutorials and documentation for these.

To cleanly mock ASP.NET MVC internals, you can use several methods. Here is an example of how to mock the Controller Context by using Rhino Mocks:

```csharp
[Test]
public void TestMethod()
{
    var request = MockRepository.GenerateStub<HttpRequestBase>();
    request.Stub(x => x.HttpMethod).Return("GET");

    var context = MockRepository.GenerateStub<HttpContextBase>();
    context.Stub(x => x.Request).Return(request);

    var controller = new YourController();
    controller.ControllerContext = new ControllerContext(context, new RouteData(), controller);

    // Call your action
    var result = controller.YourAction();

    // Make your assertions
}
```

The test method above is creating stubs for HttpRequestBase and HttpContextBase, setting up the desired behavior and then creating a new ControllerContext which is used to define the context for the YourController instance.

In case the setup code becomes too verbose or it´s repeated across various test methods, you can extract it into a helper method or into the SetUp method of your test class.

Here's an example:

```csharp
private ControllerContext CreateControllerContext(string httpMethod)
{
    var request = MockRepository.GenerateStub<HttpRequestBase>();
    request.Stub(x => x.HttpMethod).Return(httpMethod);

    var context = MockRepository.GenerateStub<HttpContextBase>();
    context.Stub(x => x.Request).Return(request);

    var controllerContext = new ControllerContext(context, new RouteData(), new EmptyController());

    return controllerContext;
}

private class EmptyController : ControllerBase
{
    protected override void ExecuteCore() { }
}
```

Then in your test method you can just call `CreateControllerContext("GET");` or `CreateControllerContext("POST");` depending on what you need.

Also, it's worth noting that using a mock framework to isolate your unit tests from external dependencies (like the ControllerContext in this case) is a good practice to make your tests more maintainable, reliable and faster.

This problem is a variant of the "Maximum Subset Product" problem which can be solved using dynamic programming techniques. The dynamic programming solution would involve keeping track of the maximum product of non-adjacent elements up to each position in the array.

Here's a simple C++ function that does this:

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

int maxSubsetProduct(vector<int> arr) {
    int n = arr.size();
    if (n == 0) return 0;

    vector<int> dp(n, 0);
    dp[0] = arr[0];

    for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
        dp[i] = max(arr[i], dp[i - 1]);
        if (i - 2 >= 0) {
            dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[i - 2] * arr[i]);
        }
    }

    return dp[n - 1];
}
```
Note: This solution assumes that all elements in the array are positive. If there could be negative values as well you might need to keep track of a "minimum product" as well, because a negative times a negative could become the maximum.

This function works by looping over the array, and at each position, it considers two possibilities: 1) including the current element in the product, (in which case the product becomes the product of the maximum up to two positions ago, times the current element), and 2) excluding the current element, (in which case the product is just the maximum till previous element) . The maximum of these two possibilities becomes the new maximum product up to the current position.

The final answer is the maximum product calculated till the end of the array.

