Design a function in PHP that takes two strings as input and returns true if the strings are an anagram of each other. Additionally, check for edge cases, such as whitespace and different letter casing. The provided code snippet has a bug; find and fix the error as well.

string1 = “Listen”
string2 = “Silent”

#Provided Code Snippet (contains an error):
```php
function checkAnagram($str1, $str2) {
    if (strlen($str1) != strlen($str2)) {
        return false;
    }

    $str1 = strtolower($str1);
    $str2 = strtolower($str2);

    $arr1 = str_split(trim($str1));
    $arr2 = str_split(trim($str2));

    sort($arr1);
    sort($arr2);

    return $arr1 === $arr1;
}
```

samples.answer:
In the provided code snippet, there is a bug in the last line where it is returning the comparison of $arr1 with itself. Instead, it should compare $arr1 with $arr2. Here is the corrected function:

```php
function checkAnagram($str1, $str2) {
    // Remove whitespace from both strings and convert to lowercase
    $str1 = strtolower(str_replace(' ', '', $str1));
    $str2 = strtolower(str_replace(' ', '', $str2));

    if (strlen($str1) != strlen($str2)) {
        return false;
    }

    $arr1 = str_split(trim($str1));
    $arr2 = str_split(trim($str2));

    sort($arr1);
    sort($arr2);

    return $arr1 === $arr2; // Compare $arr1 with $arr2 instead of $arr1 with itself
}
```

In this updated function, the whitespace removal is placed prior to the string length comparison to correctly handle strings that have differing amounts of spaces but are otherwise anagrams. Also, the comparison is made case-insensitive by applying the strtolower function before splitting and sorting the strings.

