    return 0;
}
```

This program firstly takes an input string of digits. Then it computes the aggregate numerical values via the processDigits function. This function iterates over the string in chunks of 2 characters at a time, treats these characters as digits, and then adds the result of the first digit raised to the power of the second digit to the total sum. If the string length is odd, it adds the last digit to the sum without exponentiation. Finally, the result is printed to the console.

The problem essentially asks for a list of all prime numbers from 1 to 100. In order to achieve this task, you could define a method that checks if a number is prime and then loop through all numbers from 2 to 100, printing them if they are prime.

Here is a sample implementation of the solution in C#:

```C#
public class Program
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        for(int i = 2; i <= 100; i++)
        {
            if(IsPrime(i))
            {
                System.Console.WriteLine(i);
            }
        }
    }

    public static bool IsPrime(int number)
    {
        if(number < 2) return false;

        for(int i = 2; i * i <= number; i++)
        {
            if(number % i == 0) return false;
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```
The `IsPrime` function checks whether a number is prime by attempting to divide it by all numbers less than its square root. If any of these divisions have no remainder, the number is not prime. The reason for only checking up to the square root is that a larger factor of the number would be a multiple of some smaller factor that has already been checked.

The `Main` function simply loops over the numbers 2 through 100 and prints each one that is a prime number according to the `IsPrime` function. Note that 1 is not a prime number because it has only one distinct positive divisor.

