        }
    });
});
```

Regarding data consistency, Firebase maintains data consistency automatically with its powerful APIs. With sharding and multi-regional data centers, Firestore guarantees a high level of reliability and speed. For massive data modifications, Firestore offers batch operations, transactions, and the ability to import or export data to Google's Cloud Storage. For querying, Firestore's indexing allows you to retrieve documents sorted by one or more fields without a performance loss.

This is just an example with simple data. In a real application, we would have more complex data structures, incorporate user authentication and access controls, and probably many other features as well.

In conclusion, this synchronization mechanism will provide real-time chore tasks updates, handle extensive data modification, uphold data authenticity and offer efficient query performance, leveraging Firestore's powerful capabilities.

In this program, we will simulate a Bank system scenario where we can work with two types of transactions: deposit and withdrawal. Often, while performing transactions, some errors might occur mainly in case of withdrawal when sometimes, the amount to be withdrawn exceeds the balance. For such cases, instead of just crashing the program, we need to handle such scenarios by using exceptions effectively.

Below is the code:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

class InsufficientBalanceException extends Exception {
    public InsufficientBalanceException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}

class Account {
    private double balance;

    public Account(double balance) {
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        balance += amount;
        System.out.println("Deposit successful. Current balance: " + balance);
    }

    public void withdraw(double amount) throws InsufficientBalanceException {
        if (balance < amount) {
            throw new InsufficientBalanceException("Insufficient balance. Cannot withdraw " + amount);
        }
        balance -= amount;
        System.out.println("Withdrawal successful. Current balance: " + balance);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Account account = new Account(1000);   // initial balance

        try {
            account.deposit(500);  //Increase balance by 500
            account.withdraw(2000); //Try to withdraw 2000
        } 
        catch (InsufficientBalanceException e) {
            System.err.println(e.getMessage());
        } 
        finally {
            System.out.println("End of transactions.");
        }

    }
}
```

This program creates a bank account with an initial balance of 1000. Then it tries to deposit an amount of 500 and withdraw 2000. If the amount to withdraw is more than the current balance, it will throw an InsufficientBalanceException. 

Here, we have implemented a custom exception which extends the Exception class. It signifies that the user tried to withdraw more than the existing balance, which is an erroneous condition. Hence, by using this custom exception, we are not only handling the error scenario, but also increasing the readability and understandability of the code by defining the kind of error that might occur in such a scenario. 

The try block encloses the code which might throw an exception. In case an exception is thrown, it is caught in the respective catch block, preventing the program from terminating abruptly. The finally block is used for code that must be executed regardless of whether an exception is thrown or not. Here, it is used to signal the end of transactions. 

Note: This program will require a Java compiler to run. Please run it in an Integrated Development Environment (IDE) such as Eclipse or IntelliJ, or from the command line if the Java Development Kit (JDK) is installed.

