Here is a JavaScript function that constructs Pascal's Triangle up until a user-defined limit (n).

```js
function getPascalsTriangle(n) {
    var pascalTriangle = [];
    for(var i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        pascalTriangle[i] = [];
        for(var j = 0; j <= i; j++) {
            if(j === 0 || j === i) {
                // ends of the row are always 1
                pascalTriangle[i][j] = 1;
            } else {
                // other positions are sum of the two numbers above
                pascalTriangle[i][j] = pascalTriangle[i-1][j-1] + pascalTriangle[i-1][j];
            }
        }
    }
    return pascalTriangle;
}
```

This function starts by initializing an empty array `pascalTriangle`. Then, for each row up to `n`, it creates a new array `pascalTriangle[i]`. 

For the cells in each row, it checks if the cell is at the start or end of the row using `j === 0 || j === i`, or if it's in the middle. 

If it's at the start or end, it sets the value to 1. 

If it's in the middle, it sets the value to the sum of the two cells directly above it in the previous row, as per the rule of the Pascal's triangle.

At last, the complete Pascal's Triangle is returned by the function.

Here is a Java program that generates the Fibonacci sequence, which is a sequence of numbers such that each number is the sum of the two preceding ones, usually starting with 0 and 1.

```Java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 10, t1 = 0, t2 = 1;            // We'll compute the first 10 Fibonacci numbers
        System.out.print("First " + n + " terms: ");

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
            System.out.print(t1 + " + ");      // Printing each Fibonacci number

            int sum = t1 + t2;                // Compute the next Fibonacci number
            t1 = t2;                          // Update terms to advance in the sequence
