Singleton Design Pattern is one of the Gang of Four design patterns and falls under the creational pattern group, which is concerned with object creation mechanisms. It is used when we want a class to only have a single instance exist throughout the lifetime of an application.

Here is one of the common ways to implement a Singleton pattern:

1. **Create a Singleton class:**

```java
public class Singleton {
    private static Singleton singleton_instance = null;

    // Create a private constructor to restrict the initialization of the class from outside
    private Singleton() {}

    // Method to create a new instance of Singleton class
    public static Singleton getInstance()
    {
        // Check if there is an existing instance
        if (singleton_instance == null)
          // if not, synchronize to avoid race conditions
          synchronized (Singleton.class) {
            // Double check if another thread might have initialized it
            if (singleton_instance == null)
              // if not, then create a new instance
              singleton_instance = new Singleton();
          }

        return singleton_instance;
    }
}
```

This is known as Lazy Initialized Singleton and ensures a Singleton instance is only created if it doesn't exist.

Four crucial elements are at play here:

a. **Private Constructor:** This restricts initialization of the class from any other class.

b. **Static Member:** This contains the instance of the singleton class.

c. **Static factory method:** This provides a global point of access to the Singleton object.

d. **Double Checked Locking Principle:** This is implemented with a synchronized block inside the `if` condition to make our implementation thread-safe.

**Usage:**

In your main class or any other classes, get the Singleton instance as follows:

```java
Singleton instance1 = Singleton.getInstance();
Singleton instance2 = Singleton.getInstance();

// This will return true since instance1 and instance2 are
// both instances of the Singleton class
System.out.println(instance1 == instance2);
```

A quick note here that this implementation isn't compatible with Reflection API. If multiple threads are trying to create instances at the same time, it may break the singleton property. To overcome this, you may want to use Enum to implement Singleton, which implicitly provides a serialization mechanism and guarantees against multiple instantiations, even in the face of sophisticated serialization or reflection attacks.

