Sure, here is the corresponding Perl code for the Python code provided. 

```perl
# Define the offsets for reaching neighbouring cell in a grid
my @dx = (-1, -1, -1, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1);
my @dy = (-1, 0, 1, -1, 1, -1, 0, 1);

# Verify if a given position is valid in the given grid
sub isValidMove {
    my ($x, $y, $n) = @_;
    return ($x < 0 || $y < 0 || $x >= $n || $y >= $n) ? 0 : 1;
}

# Find the minimum sum of numbers in the grid from a given position
sub findMinSum {
    my ($grid, $x, $y, $k, $n) = @_;
    return $grid->[$x][$y] if $k == 0;

    my $minSum = "inf"; # sys.maxsize replacement
    foreach my $i (0..7) {
        my ($newX, $newY) = ($x + $dx[$i], $y + $dy[$i]);
        if (isValidMove($newX, $newY, $n)) {
            my $thisSum = $grid->[$x][$y] + findMinSum($grid, $newX, $newY, $k-1, $n);
            $minSum = $thisSum if $thisSum < $minSum;
        }
    }
    return $minSum;
}

# Find the path in the grid that leads to the minimum sum
sub pathFinder {
    my ($grid, $k) = @_;
    my $n = scalar @$grid;
    my $minSum = "inf";
    foreach my $x (0..$n-1) {
        foreach my $y (0..$n-1) {
            my $thisSum = findMinSum($grid, $x, $y, $k-1, $n);
            $minSum = $thisSum if $thisSum < $minSum;
        }
    }
    return $minSum;
}
```

This block of Perl code performs the exact same logic as the Python code. 
The anonymous array `grid` is used in a similar way as the Python list of lists.

Perl doesn't have a built-in equivalent of Python's `sys.maxsize`, but floating-point numbers in Perl are able to represent positive infinity, which can be used in the same way. However, bear in mind that comparing against "inf" will always return false for `$thisSum < $minSum`. 

The `scalar` function in Perl is being used to get the length of the array. Also, unlike Python, Perl uses `$` for all variable types. One more important difference is that Perl uses `0` for false and `1` for true in the Boolean context.

Here's a C++ program that computes the longest common subsequence of two strings using dynamic programming. This approach involves creating a matrix `L` where `L[i][j]` stores the length of LCS of the substrings `string1[0..i-1]` and `string2[0..j-1]`.

```cpp
#include<bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

std::string longestCommonSubsequence(const std::string& string1, const std::string& string2) {
    int m = string1.size();
    int n = string2.size();

    int L[m + 1][n + 1];

    // Build L[m+1][n+1] in bottom up fashion
    for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) {
            if (i == 0 || j == 0)
                L[i][j] = 0;
            else if (string1[i - 1] == string2[j - 1])
                L[i][j] = L[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
            else
                L[i][j] = max(L[i - 1][j], L[i][j - 1]);
        }
    }

    // Following code is used to print LCS
    int index = L[m][n];
    char lcs[index + 1];
    lcs[index] = '\0'; // Set the terminating character

    // Start from the right-most-bottom-most corner and
    // one by one store characters in lcs[]
    int i = m, j = n;
    while (i > 0 && j > 0) {
        // If current character in string1 and string2 are same, then
        // current character is part of LCS
        if (string1[i - 1] == string2[j - 1]) {
            lcs[index - 1] = string1[i - 1]; // Put current character in result
            i--;
            j--;
            index--;
        }

        // If not same, then find the larger of two and go in the direction of larger value
        else if (L[i - 1][j] > L[i][j - 1])
            i--;
        else
            j--;
    }

    // Convert char array to string and return
    return std::string(lcs);
}

int main() {
    string string1, string2;
    cin>>string1>>string2;

    cout << "Longest Common Subsequence is: " << longestCommonSubsequence(string1, string2) << "\n";

    return 0;
}
```
This program takes two inputs, `string1` and `string2`, and outputs the longest common subsequence. If there is no common subsequence, it outputs an empty string.

Note: When you are solving a problem related to string processing, `cin` and `cout` could lead to exceed the time limit. You should use `scanf` and `printf` for input and output, respectively.

